Field requirements:
- title: a canonical, professional vulnerability name (10-26 characters, e.g. "External account authentication bypass leading to admin takeover"). If the raw name is already canonical (includes CVE, etc.), keep it or adjust only slightly; do not copy the summary.
- target: the affected host or URL, extracted from the summary/evidence; use an empty string if it cannot be determined.
- description: 1 paragraph, 2-3 sentences. Cover exactly three things—what the vulnerability is, where it occurs, and its root cause. No OWASP taxonomy dumping, no restating the fix, no generic preamble.
- module: the affected feature module (short, e.g. "external account login / unauthenticated endpoint").
- risk_analysis: 2-4 items, one sentence each; each must be a concrete consequence this vulnerability can cause on this target (who, can do what, obtains which data or privileges). Write fewer if there are no concrete consequences; avoid filler like "may affect system security".
- remediation: 2-4 items, one sentence each, concrete and actionable (which point to change, how, what to validate). Avoid empty advice like "raise security awareness" or "perform periodic security audits".
- CVSS 3.1: cvss_score (number), cvss_vector (e.g. "AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H"), cvss_metrics (the eight English metric values, e.g. {"AV":"Network","AC":"Low","PR":"None","UI":"None","S":"Unchanged","C":"High","I":"High","A":"High"}).

Do not output test_process—the reproduction section is generated by the platform from real evidence; return "test_process":[].

Output JSON only: {"title":"...","target":"...","description":["..."],"module":"...","test_process":[],"risk_analysis":["..."],"remediation":["..."],"cvss_score":0,"cvss_vector":"...","cvss_metrics":{...}}

